facts:
A **point** is named by one capital letter, such as point $A$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\path (-1.4,-0.9) rectangle (1.4,0.6);
\fill (0,0) circle (2.5pt) node[below] {$A$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

An **interval** joins two endpoints, and interval $AB$ and interval $BA$ name the same object.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw (0,0) -- (4,0);
\fill (0,0) circle (2.5pt) node[below] {$A$};
\fill (4,0) circle (2.5pt) node[below] {$B$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A **ray** starts at its first named point and continues through the second, so ray $AB$ and ray $BA$ are different.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{arrows.meta}
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,0) -- (5,0);
\fill (0,0) circle (2.5pt) node[below] {$A$};
\fill (2.5,0) circle (2.5pt) node[below] {$B$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A **line** runs forever in both directions, and line $AB$ and line $BA$ name the same line.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{arrows.meta}
\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (-1,0) -- (5,0);
\fill (0.6,0) circle (2.5pt) node[below] {$A$};
\fill (3.4,0) circle (2.5pt) node[below] {$B$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

In $\angle ABC$ the middle letter $B$ is the vertex, and the arms are rays $BA$ and $BC$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{angles}
\coordinate (B) at (0,0);
\coordinate (C) at (3.2,0);
\coordinate (A) at (2.4,2.2);
\draw (A) -- (B) -- (C);
\node[above right] at (A) {$A$};
\node[below left] at (B) {$B$};
\node[below right] at (C) {$C$};
\pic[draw, angle radius=0.8cm] {angle = C--B--A};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Matching ticks on two intervals show the intervals are equal in length.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{decorations.markings}
\tikzset{
  tickmark/.style={postaction={decorate, decoration={markings,
    mark=at position 0.5 with {\draw[black] (0pt,-4pt) -- (0pt,4pt);}}}},
}
\draw[tickmark] (0,0) -- (3.2,0);
\fill (0,0) circle (2.5pt) node[below] {$A$};
\fill (3.2,0) circle (2.5pt) node[below] {$B$};
\draw[tickmark] (0,-1.8) -- (3.2,-1.8);
\fill (0,-1.8) circle (2.5pt) node[below] {$C$};
\fill (3.2,-1.8) circle (2.5pt) node[below] {$D$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Matching arcs on two angles show the angles are equal in size.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{angles}
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (P) at (2.2,0);
\coordinate (Q) at (1.35,1.7);
\draw (P) -- (O) -- (Q);
\pic[draw, angle radius=0.7cm] {angle = P--O--Q};
\coordinate (V) at (3.9,0.2);
\coordinate (R) at (5.967,0.952);
\coordinate (S) at (4.580,2.240);
\draw (R) -- (V) -- (S);
\pic[draw, angle radius=0.7cm] {angle = R--V--S};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

